<https://example.org/wikigraph/snapshot/wikipedia/18545292/1375124090> <https://example.org/wikigraph/text> "Software development collaboration platform Not to be confused with Git or GitLab . GitHub GitHub Invertocat logo Repository of the website's official documentation, storing its source code Company type Subsidiary Website type Collaborative version control Available in English Founded February 8, 2008 (18 years ago) ( 2008-02-08 ) (as Logical Awesome LLC) Headquarters San Francisco , California, U.S. Area served Worldwide Founders Tom Preston-Werner Chris Wanstrath P. J. Hyett Scott Chacon Key people Mike Taylor (CFO) Kyle Daigle (COO) Industry Collaborative version control (GitHub) AI development tools ( GitHub Copilot ) Web host (GitHub Pages) Package repository ( npm ) Revenue $1 billion (2022) Employees 3,787 Parent Microsoft Website github .com IPv6 support No Registration Optional (required for creating and joining repositories) Users 150 million (as of May 2025 [update] ) Launched April 10, 2008 ; 18 years ago ( 2008-04-10 ) Current status Active Written in Ruby JavaScript Go C Rust ASN 36459 GitHub ( / ˈ ɡ ɪ t . h ʌ b / ⓘ ) is a proprietary developer platform that allows developers to create, store, manage, and share their code. It uses Git to provide distributed version control , and GitHub itself provides access control , bug tracking , software feature requests, task management , continuous integration , and wikis for every project. GitHub, headquartered in San Francisco , is operated by Github, Inc., a subsidiary of Microsoft since 2018. It is commonly used to host open source software development projects. As of January 2023, [update] GitHub reported having over 100 million developers and more than 420 million repositories , including at least 28 million public repositories. It is the world's largest source code host as of June 2023. [update] Over five billion developer contributions were made to more than 500 million open source projects in 2024. ^ PJ Hyett (January 22, 2010). \"New Year, New Company: As of January 1, 2010 we're operating this site as GitHub, Inc. What does that mean for you? In a word: nothing. We originally incorporated Logical Awesome LLC prior to… \" . GitHub . {{ cite web }} : CS1 maint: url-status ( link ) ^ \"Microsoft says GitHub now has a $1B ARR, 90M active users\" . TechCrunch . October 25, 2022. Archived from the original on March 14, 2023 . Retrieved March 20, 2023 . ^ \"GitHub Diversity\" . GitHub . Archived from the original on March 22, 2021 . Retrieved November 26, 2019 . ^ \"IPv6 support for cloning Git repositories · community · Discussion #10539\" . GitHub . Retrieved December 29, 2024 . ^ \"dig +noquestion +noauthority @2001:4860:4860::8888 AAAA github.com\" . isgithubipv6.live . Retrieved December 29, 2024 . ^ Muralidharan, Prakash (May 27, 2025). \"GitHub just crossed 150 million users. But that stat isn't about scale. It's about signal\" . LinkedIn . Archived from the original on May 18, 2026 . Retrieved July 22, 2026 . ^ \"GitHub\" . GitHub . Archived from the original on March 22, 2021 . Retrieved September 6, 2020 . ^ \"GitHub built a new search engine for code from scratch in Rust\" . ZDnet . Retrieved April 22, 2023 . ^ Williams, Alex. \"Github Pours Energies Into Enterprise Raises 100 Million From Power VC\" . TechCrunch . Archived from the original on September 19, 2020 . Retrieved June 25, 2017 . Andreessen Horowitz is investing an eye-popping $100 million into GitHub ^ Cite error: The named reference techcrunch was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ \"The Problem With Putting All the World's Code in GitHub\" . Wired . June 29, 2015. Archived from the original on June 29, 2015 . Retrieved June 29, 2015 . ^ \"Repository search for public repositories\" . GitHub . Archived from the original on November 5, 2020 . Retrieved June 5, 2018 . Showing 28,177,992 available repository results ^ Computer, Express (October 30, 2024). \"GitHub embraces developer choice with multi-model copilot, new app tool GitHub Spark, and AI-native developer experience\" . Express Computer . Retrieved October 31, 2024 ." .
