<https://example.org/wikigraph/snapshot/wikipedia/25652303/1374904863> <https://example.org/wikigraph/text> "Set of rules describing computer system Block diagram of a basic computer with uniprocessor CPU. Black lines indicate the flow of control signals, whereas red lines indicate the flow of processor instructions and data. Arrows indicate the direction of flow. In computer science and computer engineering , a computer architecture is the conceptual design and operational structure of a computer system that define how component parts are organized and interact to execute programs efficiently. It is often a general description that ignores precise implementation details. It covers the instruction set architecture , CPU microarchitecture , memory , and input/output systems . Computer architecture also considers tradeoffs between performance , cost, power, reliability, and security. ^ Dragoni, Nicole (n.d.). \"Introduction to peer to peer computing\" (PDF) . DTU Compute – Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science . Lyngby, Denmark. ^ Clements, Alan. Principles of Computer Hardware (Fourth ed.). p. 1. Architecture describes the internal organization of a computer in an abstract way; that is, it defines the capabilities of the computer and its programming model. You can have two computers that have been constructed in different ways with different technologies but with the same architecture." .
