<https://example.org/wikigraph/snapshot/wikipedia/440018/1336760820> <https://example.org/wikigraph/text> "Style of dynamic typing in object-oriented programming Type systems General concepts Type safety Strong vs. weak typing Major categories Static vs. dynamic Manifest vs. inferred Nominal vs. structural Duck typing Minor categories Abstract Dependent Flow-sensitive Gradual Intersection Latent Refinement Substructural Unique Session v t e In computer programming , duck typing is an application of the duck test —\"If it walks like a duck and it quacks like a duck, then it must be a duck\"—to determine whether an object can be used for a particular purpose. With nominative typing , an object is of a given type if it is declared as such (or if a type's association with the object is inferred through mechanisms such as object inheritance ). With duck typing, an object is of a given type if it has all methods and properties required by that type. Duck typing may be viewed as a usage-based structural equivalence between a given object and the requirements of a type. ^ \"Glossary — Python 3.7.1 documentation\" . docs.python.org . Archived from the original on 25 June 2020 . Retrieved 8 November 2018 . ^ \"Python Duck Typing - Example\" . Techie Hours . 28 June 2020. Archived from the original on 31 March 2022 . Retrieved 26 July 2020 ." .
